{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":420278,"duration_api_ms":420278,"is_error":false,"result":"Checking what survived the interrupted download.Plex file is actually the Regular variable font, so I'll use Archivo Black for the card text. Checking whether a font-parsing library already exists locally before any network step.Not available locally; trying a short, bounded install of `opentype.js` into `/tmp` (used only for one-off path generation, never a repo dependency).Installed. Now generating a TS module of pre-positioned SVG paths for the card's fixed text plus an A–Z/0–9 glyph table for initials.Glyphs generated (24 KB). Now writing the pure origin helper.Now the sharp renderer, mirroring `kiss-card.ts` geometry.Now wiring both image routes (`/c/$code` and the duplicate `/api/og/$code`) to the renderer.Cross-importing between route files can confuse TanStack's code-splitting, so I'll move `shareCardResponse` into the renderer module and dynamically import it from both routes.Now a test for the origin helper, following the existing `src/lib/phone.test.ts` pattern.Tests pass and typecheck is clean. Now rendering sample cards directly with sharp to eyeball the output.Just the script's module format (it's in `/tmp` with a CJS package.json). Renaming to `.mts`.`sharp` resolves from the repo, not `/tmp`; moving the check script inside the repo temporarily (it won't be committed).All variants come out 1200×630 JPEG (~30 KB). Let me look at them.Cards render correctly with the photo, with Latin initials, and with the lips fallback for Hebrew names. Now removing the temp script and running `vite build` with `DATABASE_URL` unset.Build passes. Checking that sharp's native binary was traced into the Vercel function (not bundled) and isn't in the client bundle.sharp is externalized with its native binary traced into the function; nothing leaked to the client assets. Confirming the lockfile carries the Linux binaries that Vercel's build will install.Lockfile has